module Wikifn.Unicode.Case

open Wikifn.Primitive.Kernel

(*
  Unicode case conversion, as Wikifunctions means it.

  Z10047 to lowercase and Z10018 to uppercase have four implementations on the
  wiki - JavaScript and Python for each - and all four are the same thing: the
  Unicode Default Case Conversion algorithm for the *root* locale. Not simple
  1:1 mapping, and not locale-tailored. That matters, because the corpus's own
  testers probe exactly the cases where those differ:

    tschuess with a sharp s uppercases to a double S, which is longer than it
    started; the Turkish dotted capital I lowercases to i plus a combining dot;
    a Greek capital sigma lowercases to a *final* sigma at the end of a word and
    a medial one elsewhere; Latin Extended-C maps back into Latin Extended-B
    across a gap of ten thousand codepoints; and Georgian maps to Mtavruli with
    two holes in the middle of the block.

  Nine of the fifteen testers for these two functions falsify an ASCII-only
  implementation, and several falsify an offset-only one. So this is a real
  table, not a shortcut.

  It stays checkable because it is stored as runs rather than pairs. A run is a
  span of source codepoints whose targets sit at a constant offset, with a
  stride so that the alternating upper/lower pattern of Latin Extended-A is one
  run rather than fifty. 205 runs cover all 1,478 uppercase mappings and 193
  cover all 1,487 lowercase ones; the largest term here is 8 KB, against the
  64 KB a single F* term can be checked at. Targets are computed as
  `tlo + (c - lo)` rather than by a signed offset, so everything stays `nat` and
  no subtraction has to be justified.

  WHAT THIS CLAIMS: for every codepoint, the root-locale Unicode mapping,
  including the unconditional multi-character expansions and the one conditional
  rule the root locale has (Final_Sigma).

  WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: any locale tailoring. Turkish and Azeri dotless i, and
  Lithuanian accent retention, are deliberately absent - the wiki's own
  implementations do not do them either, and tester Z12388 requires their
  absence. A function that wants Turkish casing has to say so itself.

  AND ONE HONEST CAVEAT: the tables here were generated from the host
  JavaScript engine's Unicode data rather than from a vendored copy of the
  Unicode Character Database. Those two disagree - the wiki's own JavaScript and
  Python implementations disagree with each other on 28 codepoints, in the Garay
  block and a handful of Latin Extended-D, purely from version skew. No tester
  in the corpus touches any of them. Generating from a pinned UCD, and stating
  the version as part of the claim, is the remaining work.
*)

(* A case rule covers a contiguous or stride-2 run of source codepoints whose
   target is at a constant offset: source lo, lo+stride, ... up to hi maps to
   tlo, tlo+stride, ... *)
type case_rule = codepoint & codepoint & nat & codepoint
type case_range = codepoint & codepoint
type case_expansion = codepoint & list codepoint

